text:
Good:
(1) The room is clean;
(2) Location is good, but I think all the hotels in downtown area are good too;
(3) Some staffs are friendly;
Bad:
(1) The parking is expensive, should have a discount for hotel customer;
(2) The breakfast is not worth the money and waiters are not friendly;
(3) Difficult to find the way out of the parking lot;
(4) Some staffs are not friendly;
(5) It's noisy in the morning even we stayed at 12th floor;
(6) Too many options on TV, I didn't figure out how to watch a normal TV until 2nd day;
(7) No refrigerator for customer use in room;
(8) Wireless should be free or lower the cost.

I stayed at the HGI on the recommendation of a colleague. Also, it is one of my company's approved hotels for travel. I wasn't sure what to expect, but I was pleasantly surprised. 
Pros:
1. Very courteous staff. They called me by name on the second day. Always greeted me when I arrived, and were very accommodating; 
2. The hotel offers a 'breakfast made to order'...essentially, you can order whatever you like for a hot breakfast. I always had egg white omelets and they were consistently very good. They also have a variety of juices, yogurts and wonderful FRESH FRUIT (pineapple, water melon, cantaloupe, etc.);
3. They have fresh baked cookies, coffee and fresh fruit in the afternoons;
4. The room service staff was very efficient and polite;
5. LOCATION - if you have a 24 Hour Fitness membership, they have one literally next door and it is hardly busy, EVEN DURING PEAK HOURS. Additionally, the staff there are exceptionally friendly. I recommend JT for a personal training session. He is awesome! Also, across the freeway, you have a variety of restaurants as well as grocery stores.
Cons:
1. The "pool" is extremely small and located right next to the entry. So, you really are on display. Additionally, it didn't appear to be very clean;
2. The maid consistently forgot to leave hand/bath soap, so I was constantly having to ask for soap;
3. The last couple of days, I noticed ants in the bathroom. It wasn't like an infestation, but it was bothersome;
4. I was on the non-freeway side of the hotel, but still could hear considerable noise;
5. The hall light comes in under the door, so I placed a towel there each night.
I am returning for work this upcoming week and planning on staying there again.

Stayed there for a week long, was assigned to a very nice room on the 8th floor; Hotel location is perfect for those who like walking to the major city attractions;
A few hi-cups to mention in regard to wifi connection and car rental, all of which professionally and promptly solved; To mention are the fantastic Concierge services overall!!
I can only recommend this hotel, and would certainly return on my next visit to SF.

I stayed at this hotel on the nights of 11-23 and 24 (Thanksgiving) and found conditions to be less than acceptable. The following were the issues:
-Sticky substance on the bathroom floor which I cleaned up;
-I was guaranteed a queen sized bed, but I believe, the bed in the room was standard size. When I complained I was told the room had a European queen sized bed;
-Electronic cable and parts were dumped in the hallway in a heap where they remained for my entire stay;
-Mold on shower wall;
-Replacement towel had a huge yellow stain on it;
-On final day a hotel employee knocked on the door once and then attempted to enter the room fortunately the security device was in place; and
-When I complained to Holiday Inn customer service, they responded with the usual drivel about how important I was as a member of their priority club. They said they would forward my complaint to the hotel management. Needless to say, I have heard nothing from the hotel management.

I stayed at The Strand in NYC this week, and it is a safe, sumptuous, clean, first-rate hotel in central Manhattan, one you could return to again & again, and know you will have a fine stay. My room was on the 9th floor, and street noise was negligible (& with heavy curtains drawn, not an issue at all). As others have mentioned, the front desk staff is extremely friendly (& knew me by name, Ms. ______, each time I came or went!). The mid-size room size is typical for an upscale New York hotel, with all the amenities: beautiful decor, granite-&-glass bathroom, two robes, turn-down service (of the beds), an in-room safe, privacy/clean-my-room "buttons" (instead of a hanging sign for the outdoor doorknob). The smallish roof terrace bar and its view are awesome; I just went up the elevator to look at it, but would definitely make sure I dawdled with a drink up there next time. Breakfast is in the full-size hotel restaurant/bar just off the lobby, and it was quickly served (it is not just a buffet); my room rate did include a complimentary continental breakfast, which included a nice amount of mixed fruit, four mini-pastries (1 croissant, 1 roll, 1 danish, and 1 muffin), plus butter and jam (nicely presented), and coffee (normally priced at $18 -- ok, so it's New York). Since I was visiting a family member in the city for late brunch, I did not mind the lack of protein (& could have ordered eggs, omelets & more for a charge). Free wifi (in the room, & I assume throughout the hotel) was also available, using a password provided during check-in. A small Business Center was useful for copying & printing out things. The TV and the remote worked fine, check-in & check-out were quick, easy & accurate, porters and the usual carts were available to help with my luggage, and a nearby garage cost $43/day (again, it's NYC)(on 36th, almost to 5th Ave). The bed was very very comfortable, & lovely. The only reason I'm not giving The Strand five stars (though I might give it 4&1/2) is that the lobby is small (decorated quite well, but small) and a few minor things that are important to me:
(1) I would have preferred the room service dinner for two be delivered on a rolling table (my computer & devices and various cords were already filling up the desk, & had to be cleared to make room for the food)(but to be fair, the two dinner salads we ordered came in less than 30 minutes & were really quite good);
(2) the lighting was good, and in particular the desk lamp and the lamp between the beds were large and stable, but I would have preferred for them to be on a base with at leas two electrical outlets: I had to search high & low to find enough wall outlets for my computer, phone, & iPad;
(3) there was a small horizontal stain on one of the window drapes (4" long?), perhaps not a big deal, but that and the small lobby cost The Strand a fifth star from me;
(4) the desk pen provided was starting to lose ink, so it was frustrating to write with;
(5) view from the room is nothing special, just lots of buildings.
Lastly, I want to add here that I have no idea what "tomuchtravel" was talking about, in his/her one-star review of The Strand; I have stayed in hotels all over NYC, and The Strand is definitely a very nice, top-drawer hotel. To call its area "desolate" is bizarre: The Strand is in the Garment District, between 5th & 6th Avenues, in practically the very center of New York! Cabs & people are everywhere! It's a terrific base for a stay in the city. I recommend it. (It can be pricey, but I used an on-line discount service, and was practically grateful I could find such a nice hotel for $340/night during Fashion Week; in contrast, I stayed in an equally nice hotel, nearby, in late August, when the city was dead, and only paid $200/night.)
